1. Split the sorted array into two consecutive groups of n elements each. The product is (range of first group) * (range of second group). So for all possible splits (i from 0 to n, since the first group starts at i and has n elements. But wait, in a sorted array of 2n elements, the first group can start at i where 0 <= i <= n. Because if i is n, then the first group is i= n to i+n-1 = n + 3-1 = 2n-1? Wait, wait n=3, 2n=6. So for i from 0 to 3.

