Potential edge cases to consider:

- Blocks that are placed in such a way that multiple lines are cleared after each drop.

- Vertical blocks that are cleared in the same line.

- A block that fills the last cell of a row, causing it to be cleared. 

Another edge case: a vertical block of length 5 placed in a column, which after placement fills 5 rows, each of which is part of a filled row. For example, after placing, all 5 rows are filled. 

But with the code, when the block is placed, the grid will have 5 rows. Then, all 5 rows are filled. So the code will remove all 5 rows, and the heights will be reduced by 5 for each column. 

But the vertical block only affects one column. So after processing, the heights for that column would be 5 (new_height) and then reduced by 5 (since all 5 rows are filled and removed). So heights[c] becomes 0. 

