So the task is, for each possible d (from 1 to n), determine if there's a way to replace the '?'s such that for all i, s[i] and s[i+d] (when they exist) are equal. Then collect all such possible d's and output them in sorted order.

The problem is that for each possible d, we need to check if it's possible to assign the '?'s so that for all i, s[i] == s[i+d]. But how do we model that?

Let's think about how to check for a particular d. For each i, the positions j where j = i mod d. All positions in the same equivalence class modulo d must have the same character. So for example, if d is a candidate period, then all characters in positions 0, d, 2d, ... must be the same. Similarly, positions 1, 1+d, 1+2d, ... must be the same, and so on for each residue mod d. But since the string has length n, not all residues will have the same number of elements. So for each residue r in 0..d-1, the group of positions r, r+d, r+2d, etc., must all be assigned the same character (either V or K), with the existing non-? characters in those positions possibly constraining the choices.

