So the square's side length is the minimum of the selected planks' heights multiplied by the number of planks selected. Wait, no. Wait, no. Wait, the square's side is the minimum of the heights (since that's the limiting height) and the number of planks (since that's the width). Because the square has to have equal width and height. So the maximum possible square side is the minimum between the number of planks selected and the minimum height among them.

Wait, no. Let me think. Suppose we select k planks. The width of the combined structure is k (each plank is 1 unit wide). The height is the minimum of the a_i's of those planks. To form a square, the side length can't be larger than the width (k) or the height (min_height). So the maximum possible square is the minimum of these two. So for each possible selection of k planks, the square side is min(k, min_a). And we need to find the maximum possible value over all possible k and selections.

So the problem reduces to, for each possible k (number of planks selected), find the maximum possible min(k, a_i) where a_i is the minimum of the k largest planks.

