An efficient way to generate all possible non-empty subsets of indices where no two are consecutive.

This can be implemented using bitmask generation. For each possible bitmask (from 1 to 2^n - 1), check if the bits set are non-consecutive.

For example, for n=3, the binary representations of the subsets:

0b001 → [0] → valid.

0b010 → [1] → valid.

0b100 → [2] → valid.

0b101 → [0, 2] → valid.

0b011 → [0,1] → invalid.

0b110 → [1,2] → invalid.

So, for each bitmask, check that for any two set bits, they are not adjacent.

This can be done by checking that (mask & (mask << 1)) == 0. Because if any two consecutive bits are set, then mask & (mask << 1) would have a 1 in those positions.

For example, mask 0b101 → 0b101 & 0b1010 = 0 → valid.

Mask 0b011 → 0b011 & 0b110 = 0b010 → not zero → invalid.

So, the code can generate all bitmask subsets where mask & (mask << 1) == 0.

But since the sections are in a list from 0 to n-1, the mask represents the bits from 0 to n-1.

So, the code can loop from 1 to (1<<n) -1, and for each mask, check if mask & (mask << 1) == 0, and that mask has at least one bit set.

For example, in Python:

for mask in range(1, 1<<n):

    if (mask & (mask << 1)) == 0:

        # generate the subset of indices

        subset = [i for i in range(n) if (mask & (1 << i))]

So, this will generate all non-empty subsets of indices that are non-consecutive.

