Wait, no. Sereja remembers all identifiers of the rounds he took part in and all identifiers of the rounds that went simultaneously with them. So, any round that he participated in (as Div2) or that was simultaneous (either Div1 or Div2) is known. But the missed rounds are those Div2 rounds (that he could have participated in) but didn't, and aren't part of the known rounds.

So, the problem is to calculate how many Div2 identifiers are missing from the known set, considering that some might be part of simultaneous rounds that weren't in his participation or their pairs.

The key is to find all possible Div2 rounds that are not in the known set and are less than x. But how to model the possibilities?

The minimum and maximum would depend on how we can arrange the unknown rounds. For the minimum, we want to maximize the number of unknown rounds that are part of simultaneous rounds (since each such pair would contribute only one Div2 round, but the other is Div1). For the maximum, we want as many Div2 rounds as possible, meaning that the unknown rounds are all standalone Div2 rounds.

