What's the XOR property here? Oh right, if a ^ b = x, then a ^ x = b. So for each element a in the array, if there's another element equal to a ^ x, then those two can form a pair. But since the order matters (i < j), maybe we can track the count as we process the array.

So maybe we can use a hash map (like a dictionary) to keep track of the frequency of each number we've seen so far. Then, for each current element, we check how many times (current_element ^ x) has appeared before. Summing that up would give the total pairs.

