Another example: when x is 0. Then, for each element a, we need a^0 = a. So we look for the count of a in the map. So the number of pairs would be the sum of frequencies choose 2 for each a where a^0 = a (but 0 x would mean the same as a, so pairs where a appears multiple times).

Wait, but in the approach I mentioned earlier, the hash map is built by processing elements one by one. So for each element in the array, we check how many times (a ^ x) has occurred before, then add the current a to the map. This way, all previous elements are considered, ensuring i < j.

Yes. So the algorithm would be:

Initialize a dictionary to keep counts of numbers seen so far.

Initialize the result to 0.

For each number in the array:
    result += count.get(number ^ x, 0)
    update the count for the current number in the dictionary.

This should work. Because for each element, the elements before it are already in the map, so when we compute (current ^ x), if it exists in the map, those elements are before current, so their indices are smaller, forming valid pairs.

