Oh right! So the expectation of x^k for a binomial distribution. That's the main problem now. How to compute E[x^k] when x is binomial(n, p). 

I remember that for moments of binomial distributions, there's a way to compute them using generating functions or dynamic programming. Let's think about the generating function approach. The moment generating function for x is (1-p + p e^t)^n. Then the k-th moment can be found by taking the k-th derivative of this function evaluated at t=0. But calculating the k-th derivative for k up to 5000 might be computationally intensive. Maybe there's a better way.

