3. Sum all these terms and return the sum modulo 998244353.

But how to compute (n choose t) when n can be up to 998244352 and t up to 5000?

Wait, the binomial coefficient (n choose t) is n*(n-1)*...*(n-t+1)/(t!) ). But when t is up to 5000, and n can be up to 1e9, but modulo 998244353, which is a prime.

Wait, but 998244353 is a prime. So to compute (n choose t) modulo 998244353 for t up to 5000, we can compute the numerator as the product of n*(n-1)*...*(n-t+1), then multiply by the modular inverse of t! modulo 998244353.

