So each term is:

C(n, t) * S(k, t) * fact[t] * (inv_m)^t mod MOD

But fact[t] is t! mod MOD, so S(k, t) * fact[t] is the same as S(k, t) * t! mod MOD. Which is the same as the number of surjective functions from a k-element set to a t-element set. Because S(k, t) * t! is the number of ways to partition the k elements into t non-empty subsets and then assign each subset to a distinct element (i.e., surjective functions).

So, putting this together, the term is (n choose t) * (S(k, t) * t! ) * (inv_m)^t.

But how to compute this?

Let me outline the steps:

1. Compute inv_m = pow(m, MOD-2, MOD).

2. Precompute Stirling numbers S(k, t) for all t from 0 to k. Since k can be up to 5000, this is feasible.

